Partial trust restoration is a useful way to describe the checkpoint problem.

A checkpoint is not uniformly trustworthy. Tool outputs, retrieved documents, model state, pending actions, and credentials can all have different trust status. Treating the whole checkpoint as either valid or invalid loses too much information, but letting unverified state influence execution is worse.

The safest boundary is verification before influence:

- verify the checkpoint envelope before loading it into executable graph state;
- classify each component by trust level and authority scope;
- quarantine components that cannot be verified;
- require revalidation before any component can drive a side-effecting node;
- emit a decision record when a checkpoint is accepted, partially restored, or rejected.

That gives the framework a stronger invariant than "the user checked the checkpoint." The graph runtime can enforce that untrusted state cannot silently become trusted execution context.

A useful conformance suite would include:

- valid checkpoint, all components restored;
- valid envelope, one component stale -> partial restore;
- valid tool output, corrupted agent state -> quarantine state only;
- pending side-effect from prior checkpoint -> require revalidation;
- checkpoint written under compromised identity -> reject or quarantine;
- credential-bearing state present -> hard block unless stored as a reference, not raw credential material.

That would make trust-gated checkpoints operationally testable instead of only a policy recommendation.

@dlindb yes — that's the right framing. The pre-interrupt task outputs are saved in the checkpoint, but the `is_replaying` flag (set by the pre-1.1.9 `checkpoint_id`-based check) told the pregel loop to re-execute the tasks rather than hydrate them from checkpoint state. So the checkpoint ‘had’ the tool call response, but the loop asked the model to generate a new one instead of consuming the stored result.

For anyone who hits this in production with LangGraph 1.1.x and can’t upgrade immediately: the workaround is to pass `input=None` instead of `Command(resume={...})` when you want true time-travel replay semantics, and reserve `Command(resume=...)` for interrupt-continuation semantics. The root bug was that the two code paths shared the same `checkpoint_id`-in-config detection, collapsing two distinct operations (replay vs. resume) into the same branch.

Confirming sardismart’s finding — 1.1.9 fixes this. We’re running 1.1.9 with AsyncPostgresSaver and the `interrupt()` → `Command(resume=...)` pattern in a FastAPI backend with no recurrence of the replay false-positive.

@dlindb — the expectation should be idempotency at the tool layer, but it has to be the right kind. A check against graph state doesn't work because the re-dispatched worker starts with a fresh view of checkpoint state that may predate the original execution completing. The guard needs to live in durable storage outside the graph, keyed to the logical operation before dispatch.
That's what SafeAgent enforces: POST /claim with a stable request_id (derived from tool arguments, not attempt metadata) atomically inserts PENDING before execution. Any re-dispatched worker hitting the same request_id gets SKIP immediately — no re-execution, no state reference check needed. The two-phase claim (PENDING → COMMITTED) handles the crash-mid-call window so a false COMMITTED never masks a missing side effect.
Live at safeagent-production.up.railway.app if you want to test the flow directly.

@dlindb — I don't own DashClaw so I can't speak to its implementation details, but @ucsandman shipped v2.14.0 with durable execution finality (ucsandman/DashClaw#105) — that's the closest public reference for how the decision context and outcome state are tracked on that side.

The general pattern for state authority checks in this stack: the decision_id anchors the state reference at decision time. Any downstream execution that carries the same decision_id can be validated against the recorded decision context — if the state has advanced past what the decision was derived from, the execution is stale. Whether that check is a hash comparison, checkpoint ID, or version number is an implementation detail, but the linking key is decision_id → request_id → action_ref, all content-addressed from the same inputs.

Your framing of 'execution gate' before the claim is the right architectural slot for it. SafeAgent's /claim is the execution gate for idempotency — the state authority check would be a pre-condition before /claim is issued, not inside it.

This looks like a classic checkpoint/retry boundary problem: the graph can recover execution state, but the tool side effect is not owned by an idempotency receipt.

For long-running tool calls, I would want the runtime to persist an `in_flight_tool_call` record before dispatch:

- node/run id;
- tool name and normalized arguments hash;
- idempotency key;
- external resource target;
- dispatch timestamp and lease/heartbeat;
- completion state;
- result reference;
- retry policy.

Then checkpoint recovery can distinguish `not dispatched`, `dispatched but unknown`, `completed`, and `safe to retry`. Without that state, a replay from checkpoint can look logically correct while duplicating payments, emails, writes, or API posts.

The broader enterprise pattern is that tool execution needs receipts, not just checkpoints. Checkpoints restore control flow; receipts preserve side-effect ownership.

I have been mapping this as part of an Enterprise AI OS architecture around action receipts, capability grants, and recovery semantics: https://github.com/hegu-1/enterprise-ai-os-architecture

A working workaround:

```
from typing import Annotated, Any

from bson import ObjectId
from langgraph.checkpoint.serde import jsonplus
from langgraph.checkpoint.serde.jsonplus import EXT_CONSTRUCTOR_SINGLE_ARG, _msgpack_enc, ormsgpack
from langgraph.checkpoint.serde.jsonplus import (
    _msgpack_default as _msgpack_default_langgraph
)
from pydantic import BaseModel
from pydantic.json_schema import JsonSchemaValue
from pydantic_core import core_schema


def _msgpack_default(obj: Any) -> str | ormsgpack.Ext:
    if isinstance(obj, ObjectId):
        return ormsgpack.Ext(
            EXT_CONSTRUCTOR_SINGLE_ARG,
            _msgpack_enc(
                (obj.__class__.__module__, obj.__class__.__name__, str(obj)),
            ),
        )
    return _msgpack_default_langgraph(obj)


setattr(jsonplus, "_msgpack_default", _msgpack_default)


class PydanticObjectIdAnnotation:
    @classmethod
    def validate_object_id(cls, v: Any, handler) -> ObjectId:
        if isinstance(v, ObjectId):
            return v

        s = handler(v)
        if ObjectId.is_valid(s):
            return ObjectId(s)
        else:
            raise ValueError("Invalid ObjectId")

    @classmethod
    def __get_pydantic_core_schema__(cls, source_type, _handler) -> core_schema.CoreSchema:
        assert source_type is ObjectId
        return core_schema.no_info_wrap_validator_function(
            cls.validate_object_id,
            core_schema.str_schema(),
            serialization=core_schema.to_string_ser_schema(),
        )

    @classmethod
    def __get_pydantic_json_schema__(cls, _core_schema, handler) -> JsonSchemaValue:
        return handler(core_schema.str_schema())


PydanticObjectId = Annotated[ObjectId, PydanticObjectIdAnnotation]


class DummyModel(BaseModel):
    object_id: PydanticObjectId


dummy_model = DummyModel(object_id=ObjectId())

```

