@anneheartrecord's source-level analysis is the most important comment in this thread — the actual serde path uses ormsgpack, not the `dumpd()` JSON the benchmarks measured, and checkpoint bytes don't enter the prompt path. That changes the conversation from "serialization is broken" to "structural validation is missing," which is a harder and more interesting problem.

The tool_call/ToolMessage pairing gap is real and I've seen the same pattern in a different context. When you store agent conversation state with any form of message pruning or decay (dropping old low-relevance turns to bound storage), the pairing invariant is the first thing that breaks silently. You remove a ToolMessage because it's old and low-scored, but its parent AIMessage with `tool_calls` stays because it had other content. The model then sees a dangling reference and hallucinates tool results.

For anyone implementing their own message windowing: we track referential integrity edges (AIMessage.tool_calls → ToolMessage.tool_call_id) as a lightweight graph alongside the message list, so pruning a message cascades to its dependents. Implementation runs at the storage layer — the tiered storage config shows the architecture: https://github.com/Dakera-AI/dakera-deploy/blob/main/docker/docker-compose.yml

After reading the source-level breakdown in this thread, I would narrow the problem the same way: serializer pluggability already exists on the checkpoint path; the missing contract is referential integrity when message state gets mutated over time.

The operator case that keeps biting is bounded-history pruning. A graph trims an old `ToolMessage` because it looks low-value, but leaves the parent `AIMessage.tool_calls` alive because that turn also contains user-visible text. On the next model turn the state is syntactically valid but semantically corrupt: the model sees a dangling tool reference and either hallucinates the missing result or reissues work.

So before expanding the serializer surface area, I would lock in a pair-preserving mutation contract:

1. if pruning drops an `AIMessage.tool_calls` entry, the matching `ToolMessage` must also be removed or both sides must be replaced by one summarized receipt;
2. if pruning drops a `ToolMessage`, the parent tool-call reference must be rewritten or removed in the same operation;
3. checkpoint round-trip, reducer output, and subgraph handoff should all be able to run a strict-mode validator that rejects orphaned or dangling pairs.

The minimal regression matrix I would want is:
- `add_messages` + `RemoveMessage` cannot create orphaned tool results silently;
- checkpoint save/load preserves valid pairings and rejects broken ones in strict mode;
- context-window trimming either cascades both sides of a pair or emits a summarized replacement record;
- subgraph handoff cannot expose a tool result without its causal tool-call edge.

That keeps the issue anchored on the correctness failure the thread has converged on: not generic serialization cost, but silent corruption of agent-visible history once state is trimmed, summarized, or handed across boundaries.

This bug is a real blocker for production approval-gate patterns. We use `interrupt()` inside a loop for multi-step human review (reviewer can push back, add a comment, and the agent re-prompts with revised context) — exactly the pattern the docs show. The exponential replay behavior isn't just confusing; it means each resume call re-executes all the node logic from the beginning, which causes duplicate side effects (API calls, DB writes, log entries) if any occur before the interrupt in the loop body.

The per-site fix scoped out by @hipvlady and @clmoon2 is the right design. The `(co_filename, f_lineno)` key is stable across checkpoint/restore because it's source-level, not runtime — good call.

One practical note for anyone building approval gates while waiting for the fix: **avoid any side effects before `interrupt()` in a loop body**. Put all work after the `interrupt()` call or in a separate node. The replay will re-run them N times otherwise. Restructuring to:

```python
def approval_node(state):
    while True:
        decision = interrupt({"prompt": build_prompt(state), "context": state["context"]})
        if decision["approved"]:
            break
        # update context for next prompt — this runs N times on replay too
        state = {**state, "context": apply_feedback(state["context"], decision["feedback"])}
    return {"approved": True}
```

is safer than calling external services before the interrupt on each iteration.

