Yes, that proposed-call envelope framing is the right separation: keep the hook small, but make the object being decided stable enough that policy, interrupt, retry, and result handling all refer to the same thing.

I would make one distinction explicit in the model: proposed envelope versus effective envelope.

The proposed envelope is what enters the hook:

- tool_call_id and attempt id;
- tool name and canonical args digest;
- graph, run, thread, and selected state refs when available;
- tool classification and policy profile version;
- resume or retry context.

The decision should bind to that proposed envelope. If the hook allows the call unchanged, execution proceeds only if the envelope is still identical at the final pre-call check.

If the hook modifies args or any execution-relevant field, that should mint an effective envelope with its own digest. The decision record then needs to bind both the proposed envelope and the effective envelope. That avoids treating modify as a soft annotation on the original call; it is a new execution object with a visible parent.

For interrupt/resume, I would make the rule equally strict: resume can consume the prior decision only when the proposed envelope is still identical. If the args, selected state digest, policy profile, or attempt context changed while suspended, the resumed call should re-enter the hook instead of inheriting authority.

For retry, I would separate two cases:

- retry same envelope under an explicitly retryable decision;
- retry changed envelope, which requires a fresh decision.

That gives tests a crisp shape:

- block emits a terminal outcome and never calls the tool;
- allow executes only the envelope that was authorized;
- modify records proposed and effective envelope digests;
- interrupt resumes only the same envelope;
- retry same envelope preserves the decision id only when retryable;
- retry changed envelope forces revalidation;
- tool exception still emits a terminal outcome linked to the decision.

This keeps the first public API small while preventing the audit trail from becoming ambiguous at the exact places production agents usually drift: modification, suspension, retry, and failed execution.

